Because I''m seeing way too many confused and frustrated with their lack of results.

Where in reality, 99% of them are focusing on the wrong things.

Calories first.

Once you understand your calories, results will come.

And you can get fancy with it if you want.

But calories first.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('1c0d9f7c-2386-573e-aa9d-e0fb17818c22', 'sbw', 18, 'Traction vs Compression based exercises', 'https://youtu.be/TGoe57kgh7Y', 'Some exercises will wear you down more than others

(for instance, heavy barbells squats put way more stress on you than seated bicep curls)

This is called the ''orthopedic cost'' of an exercise

(how much ''stress'' the exercise puts you under)

If you''re like me, you want to get strong, look good, and feel amazing - but you don''t want to wreck your body in doing so

You want to be fit for LIFE

That''s why you need to keep your spine healthy

One of the ways I program this into my clients'' routines is by using more ''traction'' based exercises than ''compression'' based exercises

In other words, more ''heal'' and less ''havoc''', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('bdfff04c-8ab2-5220-acf9-b321da39e7b4', 'sbw', 19, 'Everything you need to know about Macro''s (for now)', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QCsIokXIsCE', 'Carbs are not the devil

Fats don''t make you fat

And you don''t need absurd amounts of protein to get fit

All of the above are misconceptions that come from people not understanding what macros are.

And I know, macros can be a scary word.

But it''s really not hard. And once you understand them, you''re way more likely to stick to your meal plan.

Therefore, today''s video...

- Is only 1 minute and 52 seconds long
- Has a song in it
- Will teach you

-1- where macros come from
-2- what the 3 macros are
-3- what purpose each macro serves', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('7d5725e0-9107-54af-a71c-66799754dc7f', 'sbw', 20, 'You need more magnesium (here''s how)', null, 'Aside from potassium, one of the most underrated minerals nobody talks about is magnesium.

And just like potassium, everybody''s deficient in it.

As an indirect result, more and more Americans are fat

Moodier than ever

Emotionally unstable

And worst of all, our testosterone levels suck

This is causing our muscles to wither away, bones to become brittle, and our bodies just keep on getting pudgier.

Magnesium helps with all that

I''ve been taking magnesium for almost a decade. It helps you:

- turn food into energy,
- maintain your nerves,
- fight depression
- repair muscle
- stay ''explosive''
- regulate your blood sugar, heart health, inflammation, bones, migraines, quality of sleep, libido, etc.

You just need to get it in.

The problem is though, magnesium is a major mineral

So unlike ''trace'' minerals like zinc or iron, you need lots of it to be effective.

Here are 3 ways to get it in:

-1- Food
-2- Supplements
-3- Transdermally (through your skin)

-1- FOOD

Foods that are high in magnesium are:

Spinach
Almonds
Yogurt
Cacao Nibs
Potatoes
Steak
Avocado

-2- SUPPLEMENTS (pills)

If you go this route, get the BioOptimizers brand of Magnesium pills.

I don''t get paid for referring you, but they''re the best (I''ve tried many).

Their ''Magnesium Breakthrough'' contains the 7 best forms of magnesium.

The brands at your grocery store or Costco use the ''oxide'' form of magnesium, which is like the Burlington Coat Factory of magnesium.

BioOptimizers = Hugo Boss. Find it online.

-3- TRANSDERMALLY

The easiest way to get magnesium through your skin is in a ''float tank''.

These are hot tubs filled with thousands of pounds of epsom salt. Look up ''float tank'' near you, then you can book sessions that range from 1 hour to all night long.

As you float on the surface, your body soaks up the magnesium.

And as an added benefit, you''re usually in a pitch black environment, so it''s a cool way to de-stress.

You can also use a spray at home.

But I''ve tried that, and it sucks. You''ll be spraying your whole body every night before bed.

Then you''re sticky, and your lovelife is canceled.

Of all the ways, I''d say food is best. Because food = a daily habit.

Then add in a float tank biweekly or even weekly.

This way your magnesium levels will be ''topped off'', and that''s hard to do with food alone.

So... I truly hope you book yourself a float tank session, eat more almonds and spinach, or get those supplements.

And while I do appreciate you reading my emails.

If you still decide not to take magnesium, then whatever man, enjoy your flaccid body parts and mood swings.

You need magnesium.

Don''t turn into a grumpy loser and take your magnesium.

P.S. If you can''t find a float tank near you, just get into the ocean. Swim around a little bit, let your body soak up the natural magnesium and feel the benefits almost instantly.

P.P.S. If you can''t find an ocean near you, then use the money you''re saving on mortgage payments and build a float tank

P.P.P.S. Seriously, you can build your own float tank, look it up on YouTube and go down the rabbit hole if you dare =)', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('f84b3134-9c46-58cc-a0a7-bcdd5c7d43fd', 'sbw', 21, 'Is there such a thing as working out too much?', 'https://youtu.be/x5sEqz6V10s', 'While most of us are all looking for that PERFECT workout, or that PERFECT training split, with the PERFECT calories and macros...

The truth is that your training intensity relies heavily on your very own recovery.

So the answer to this question is big...

That''s why I attack it from 2 different angles, right here in this video.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('4465b996-f87f-5915-b127-8e4a24e40219', 'sbw', 22, '5 tips for a better back squat', 'https://youtu.be/hrwVFVgaT0U', 'In today''s Strong Body Wednesday we''re talking the #1 exercise of all mankind... Squats.

I''m going to give you 5 ways to improve your back squat.

Tip #1 alone can save your back and massively improve your strength.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('da3e162f-9304-5f68-bfdf-cd5c3302a0a8', 'sbw', 23, 'High bar squats vs Low bar squats', 'https://youtu.be/gvoy3kJENSY', 'Not all weight lifting exercises are created equal

For instance, a seated bicep curl only hits your biceps

And your biceps are small compared to your whole body

Whereas a squat (or deadlift) works not only your legs - but also your back and core.

And that’s a lot of muscle in 1 movement.

The more muscle you use ''per movement'', the more you generally stimulate your ‘muscle building hormones’ (like testosterone)

That''s why I always design my clients'' workouts around a ''big lift'' of some kind

For upper body, it''s usually some kind of bench press, pullup, or incline press

For lower body it''s mostly squats and deadlifts, and sometimes heavy bulgarians (single-leg stuff)

Now with squats, there are 2 ways of doing them

1 way hits more of the ''front side'' of your legs - the other hits the back

Also, one requires much more mobility than the other

And depending on how tall you are, the length of your legs, etc - you might only be able to do 1 of the 2 (not many people can do both versions flawlessly).', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('33d8be89-bef9-53e1-8f11-56cd5838a1de', 'sbw', 24, 'Why you need to take Creatine (and how)', null, 'I can imagine that when the average woman goes into a gym for the very first time, it can be daunting.

New environment. Not sure what to do.

The first ''area'' you usually see is the free weights

And in the free weight area is where the bench press is.

The bench press is usually inhabited by the meatheads

And a lot of meatheads are on the chunky side, and they LOVE drinking protein shakes and taking creatine

There''s nothing wrong with protein shakes or creatine

But if a woman sees these faux-powerlifters drinking protein shakes and taking creatine, it''s only normal she thinks protein and creatine will make her ''bulky''

The creatine and protein however did not make these meatheads bulky

They''re bulky because they:

- eat horrible
- are in a perpetual state of ''bulking'' (because they think cutting makes them lose muscle)
- are too comfortable where they''re at in life
- have completely forgotten what it feels like to be healthy
- prioritize their bench press numbers over their health

CREATINE however, will indirectly help you lose fat and build muscle faster.

It''s one of the few supplements that actually work

Here’s the science:

Your body has 3 ''types'' of energy, and they''re like the salsa bar you see at Rubio''s.

Mild - Medium - Spicy

Mild energy comes from your blood fats (oxidative) - you use this for low intensity, long duration stuff

Medium energy comes from your glycogen - the ''fuel'' you have stored in your muscle - you use this for intense exercise

Spicy energy is called your ''phosphatic'' energy. This is what you use for explosive stuff like sprints, or lifting weight.

Creatine gives you spicy energy.

(for the nerds among us - it does that through adenosine triphosphate)

It basically allows more water into your muscle cells, making you slightly more powerful.

And this helps you burn fat because...

The more weight you lift, the more muscle you damage

The more muscle you damage, the more calories it requires to ''regrow'' that muscle

And the more calories you need to keep that new muscle alive

As a result, you can eat more without getting fat

The problem is, it''s hard to get creatine in

You basically need to eat steak all day long

A better way to ''top off'' your creatine levels is to supplement with creatine powder instead

"Where do you get creatine powder?"

Easy - get it online

And make sure you get pure creatine monohydrate

None of those fancy creatine alternatives. Your creatine should have nothing extra in it, just creatine.

And if you want the best, go for the ''creapure'' brand.

"How do you take it though?"

Easy - with water

I like taking it right after my workout (supposedly it absorbs better then)

And btw, you might hear about people ''loading'' their creatine.

This is where you take around 5 scoops per day for the first couple of days to ''saturate'' your muscle.

This is unnecessary. Just start taking it regularly and you''ll eventually saturate yourself with it.

Then just stay consistent. But drink extra water.

Just because the meatheads love it doesn''t mean it''ll turn you into a meathead.

Add some creatine to your staple. I guarantee your lifts will go up.

Michael

P.S. Just fyi - ''chunky'' or ''bulky'' means nothing more than you having muscle underneath a thick layer of fat. If you don''t want to be ''chunky'' or ''bulky'', get your calories right and find ways to enjoy your food every day.

.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('8c1b8fbb-06ed-5fa7-8608-b2b4f63ce87f', 'sbw', 25, 'How to improve your Bench Press', 'https://youtu.be/_wkGcGNP5pc', 'In your 25th SBW I want to talk everything Bench Press:

- what band exercise you should always do before your Bench
- the 1 mistake nearly everyone makes when the bar comes down
- my favorite barbell to bench press with
- how high to put the j-hooks
- how the bench is not just upper body (it''s also a leg drive)
- why you need to lift explosively on big lifts

As you know by now, even if you''re just looking to lose weight, this video is still important to you.

Proper strength training is the end-all answer to:

- build lasting muscle (not that ''pumpy'' muscle that only lasts for 30 min after your session)
- decrease risk of injuries (and stay athletic as you age)
- instant feel-good feelings and unbreakable motivation (lifting heavy stuff has that effect on you)

And what''s the #1 way to get stronger?

By lifting heavier weights

On what exercises though?

On the ''big lifts'' (squat, deadlift, bench)

... BUT - with lifting heavy, especially on free weights, form is everything.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('a7973b9c-0d65-5237-8c43-2b4b40808d0a', 'sbw', 26, 'How to move your shoulders when doing pushups', 'https://youtu.be/YhEDpekRYAc', 'When anyone asks me "how often should I workout?", I always say "move every day"

And "move" here being: getting steps in

You can actually track how many steps you take on your smartphone - and the goal should be 10,000 per day

If you want to be healthy, there really is no excuse not to hit these 10,000

10,000 steps of straight up walking

Walking is one of those things you can do every day

Outside of that, most other exercises need some ''rest'' in between

For instance when it comes to lifting weights, 72 hours is ideal

This is because you compress your spine, stress your joints, tear down muscle, etc.

Then right in between there is bodyweight training

Like pushups

If you want to get great at pushups (or pullups), I recommend you do them every other day

So 48 hours

This way you''ll still allow proper recovery, and you''ll have time to do other workouts in between those days

If you''ve been wanting to increase your pushups (or pullups), then simply do them more often =)', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('48bcaa76-79cd-58b2-9899-5a21c799b9bb', 'sbw', 27, 'How often can you repeat the same workout?', 'https://youtu.be/-dOZY1j-nhA', 'By now you probably know you can''t do the same workouts all the time right?

Your body gets used to it... progress will stop. And you''ll plateau.

Yet at the same time - you need to repeat your workouts to get better at them...

So how do you make sure you do both?

Stay consistent enough so you get better and make progress - yet switch it up often enough so your body keeps guessing.

I made you a video on this right here

In it you''ll see how I train my clients to never plateau, and always stay ''fresh'' and injury-free.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('fb4f82c0-11ea-5149-9cfa-f5f50ef20bd4', 'sbw', 28, 'The most important macro of all', 'https://youtu.be/aGS0ZY6oS2Y', 'Your ''macros'' are your Carbs, Proteins, and Fats

