insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('db258712-af88-5485-8093-b1150aca9c28', 'smf', 31, '9 traits all successful people have in common', null, 'Instead of ''reinventing the wheel''

As in, trying to find out how to be successful completely on your own...

You will be so much faster when you ''model success''

In this case, look at what successful people do daily

They are:

1... Focused and Consistent.

2... Reflective (they evaluate their actions to see which can be repeated or avoided next time)

3... Grateful (they always acknowledge the contributions of others)

4... Positive and Optimistic

5... Lifelong Learners (learning as if they''ll live forever).

6... Disciplined (they make sacrifices and always practice delayed gratification).

7... Accountable (they take 100% responsibility for where they are in life).

8... Ambitious (they set goals that are big for the long-term, while working on the smaller goals for the short-term).

9... Resilient (they don''t let past struggles define who they are. they embrace change and are open to ideas that might scare them at first).

It''s a long list

And of course, nobody is perfect

But when you start incorporating these into your life, you''ll find that you will magically attract others who live by the exact same principles.

Click here to check in for Week 31:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-32-habits-and-homework}

Once you complete your check-in, you''ll be given instructions on what to do in week 32.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('c983ee25-22fe-56b8-893b-8c49945a1eef', 'smf', 32, 'Your lows and your highs', null, 'You know this by now - but the road to success is never straight

It''s turbulent, and looks more like a stock exchange than anything regular

Average people tend to think successful people got their results without struggles or setbacks

But it''s always the successful who have endured the biggest setbacks

It''s those setbacks that:

- defined their character, because they stood back up and tried again

- gave them insight in what to do next, but then better

"Failure is never failure until it is recognized by the individual as such" - Napoleon Hill

And anyone in the world will have setbacks

It''s just that, when you set big goals and have a definite purpose in life, your lows keep getting higher

Some days you''re good

Other days you''re ''blah''

As you challenge yourself, fall down, and get back up - those ''blah'' days turn into ''meh'' days

Then you challenge yourself again - fall down, get ridiculed, and get back up - those ''meh'' days turn into ''ok'' days

This cycle repeats until your ''ok'' days are other people''s dream days

You have 100% control of where you go in life

Everything is up to you

And if your lows keep getting higher, you''re on the right track

Click here to check in for Week 32:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-33-habits-and-homework}

Once you complete your check-in, you''ll be given instructions on what to do in week 33.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('f738a86c-33ba-57de-b260-c5ef96908232', 'smf', 33, 'You''re always off', null, 'When you''re going on a flight to South Beach, you might think the nose of the airplane is pointed directly at Miami the whole flight

But this is not the case

The entire flight, your plane''s nose is pointed to the left or right of Miami

It''s always off track

It''s the navigation that''s constantly ''steering'' the plain towards Miami

The same goes for you and me, every day we walk this earth

We set goals

Work to achieve them

Then mess up

And learn how to achieve them instead

In your quest for success, you''re supposed to make mistakes

The more the better

And the bigger your mistakes, the faster you arrive

You ''correcting'' yourself after every mistake is your internal navigation telling you where to go instead

But this only works if you first ''set the destination''

Know exactly what you want (the color, the size, the shape, the date, the type, the amount)

Only then will your navigation know where to ''steer you''

Because like airplanes, we''re almost always off track

And that''s a good thing

Click here to check in for Week 33:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-34-habits-and-homework}

