insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('45c90f39-f302-56a3-9411-fd3147cf9f8e', 'smf', 34, '5 things all losers have in common', null, 'If you were to watch the news (which I don''t think you should)... then I wouldn''t blame you for thinking that society has become ''rather soft'' these days.

Most of what you see is victim-mentalities and young-people-entitlement from kids who want to get paid first and work later.

Of course... America is not like that

Everyone knows this mediocre wokeness (like ''fat acceptance'') has an expiration date

Which is why real Americans (who take ownership) are simply saying nothing

You don''t see them on the news, because they''re ''boring''

(with their healthy lifestyles, adequate water intake, and exercise regimens - ew!)

Also, there''s no money to be made in health

(millions in medication though)

The truer issue though is that there will always be more people with low self-esteem walking this world, then there will be people with high self-esteem

People with low self-esteem:

- talk smack about others behind their back

- see others succeed and write off their success as luck

- get jealous easily

- love to gossip

- live in massive scarcity

Put short, people with low self-esteem choose the easy way out

And every time they choose that easy way out, their self-esteem only drops lower

Big media sees this, and knows this is where the money''s at

So what do you see/hear on repeat?

Drama, gossip, scandals, and successful people messing up,

It''s always easier to tear someone down and stomp on their goals, than it is to build them up

But all that is weak

If you want to live a strong life - ruthlessly monitor what messaging you allow to get in your head

And do what the strong do...

Encourage others

And be happy when they win

Click here to check in for Week 34:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-35-habits-and-homework}

Once you complete your check-in, you''ll be given instructions on what to do in Week 35.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('06bc080c-0fc8-56db-a327-5a275f187fae', 'smf', 35, 'Motivation comes afterwards', null, 'Your motivation is greatly overrated

(hard work and discipline on the other hand...)

Your motivation is simply not something you can rely on

Sometimes it is nowhere to be found

And then you start doubting yourself, comparing yourself to others who are further, beating yourself up, etc.

This is why habits are crucial. along with a solid preparation.

Because whenever you don''t feel like it - remember...

Motivation often comes AFTER you take the action

(not before)

Hence the saying: Action Alleviates Anxiety

Action creates wins. And wins create more wins.

So from now on, put more energy into your ''planning'' of your day

- Surround yourself with the right environment

- Decide where you will work, where you will eat, and where you will exercise

- Kill distractions. Put your phone in another room, stop looking at screens pre bedtime

- Set definite goals and write them down with pen and paper

- Always spend money on coaching. Everyone needs someone to ''call them out'' on their excuses.

If you follow through on your schedule (even when you don''t feel like it), you''re so much more likely to make that progress

No motivation required.

Click here to check in for Week 35:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-36-habits-and-homework}

Once you complete your check-in, you''ll be given instructions on what to do in week 36.', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('115aab71-6b4f-5ded-a77b-14fab23ed944', 'smf', 36, 'How to have it all (6 steps)', null, 'All who have gotten in shape have first done a considerable amount of:

...dreaming

... hoping

... wishing

... desiring

... planning

before they achieved their results.

You can never achieve those results if you can not work yourself into a white heat of desire and actually believe you will do it.

If you need a reminder, here are the 6 steps to fire up your own belief:

-1- Set in your mind the EXACT results you want

-2- Determine EXACTLY what you''re willing to do for it

-3- Establish an EXACT deadline

-4- Plan it out and start right away (take ''imperfect action'')

-5- Write out your goals, write out your deadline, write out what you''re willing to do for it, and write out your plan in accomplishing your desire

-6- Read that statement out loud every day.

99% of people will not do these 6 steps

It''s going to be the 1% that see, feel, and believe themselves already in the possession of their results that actually accomplish it.

Woo-woo, I know

But if you do this when nobody''s watching, your reputation won''t get affected

Until you level up so much that people ask you "how you did it"

And you tell them these 6 steps

Where they will think it''s woo-woo as well

And the cycle repeats

Click here to check in for week 36: {Training Magic Link training_slug=week-37-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('1c63a845-e824-56b5-a7f8-0f4bdd0ecaca', 'smf', 37, 'Day zero', null, 'When you originally started coaching with me, you were at ''day 0''

Back then, you had a ''zero options mentality''

That means, you were SET on getting in shape

You were going to do it come hell or high water

I know this, because otherwise you wouldn''t still be with me

What''s important now - is to go back to zero

When I started SUF, I had $0

Zero clients

And bowflex dumbbells in my trunk

Stated training people in parks, in their living room

Eventually got my own location, and grew from there

Back then, in 2007 - I had ''zero options''

I was either going to make it, or perish

Fast forward to 2015, I had 3 locations

And money was flowing in

Over 1,000 clients, new house, Ducati, Aston Martin in the driveway

And I got lazy

I let my habits slip. And very slowly I started getting chubbier than I ever was.

But nobody told me anything

Because I was the owner, I probably knew what I was doing (is what most people thought)

But in reality - the mere fact of me having ''options'' made me slow down

"You work too hard"

"You should go on vacation more"

"You deserve it"

I started listening to all these mundane pieces of advice

And it wasn''t till I started editing a video from 2019 that I noticed a massive double chin - and I knew I had to get back to ''day 0''

I started walking

Eating less

The rest is history

I think most of us go through this in 1 way or another

We climb that first mountain in life, we get success

Standing at the peak, we have no more to climb. So we THINK we''re done

Then we roll back down that same mountain on the other side

We stop doing the things that got us up the mountain in the first place

Lose all momentum, and we get fat again (physically and mentally)

Then... we have that choice

Do we stay put?

Get caught in the news, let others do our thinking for us?

Get prescriptions, stand in line at the pharmacy to fight our symptoms?

Or do we take matters into our own hands?

Cure ourselves from the inside out?

Not many people will

Anno 2022, most of America (partly due to the news) is competing on who is the biggest victim

Even though 99% of our problems are self-made, it''s hip to be offended

Thing is, the only noble thing about victimhood... is overcoming it

And you do that by climbing your second mountain

This time, you go slower

But smarter too. With more poise.

Because now you know what works and what doesn''t

And you''re in it for the long haul

That''s the thing about the second mountain, it goes beyond the clouds

You will never reach its'' peak in your current lifetime

But it''s the climb that makes life worth living

Click here to check in for Week 37:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-38-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('48b316a4-f7b4-5174-b91a-50c93ed78e4d', 'smf', 38, '33% of your days will suck', null, 'I guess it can feel good to be told that ''you are enough''

And if you''re feeling overwhelmed, it can be tempting to resort to alcohol, food, or take a Xanax

But the brutal harsh reality of life is that you''re not going to feel like a winner every day.

Out of every 9 days, 3 will be awesome

3 are neutral

And on the other 3 you would rather stay in bed

This is normal, because remember:

1 - Social media is not real

2 - Nobody is a robot.

As humans we have limits to our capacities.

And increasing our capacity is a lifelong pursuit that you''ll never be done with.

Like I said last week, you''re either climbing the mountain of life or falling off of it.

Sometimes though, that climb gets REALLY tough

And you don''t feel like carrying on

This is where your discipline will trump motivation

If you want to succeed in your endeavours - you must do what needs to be done regardless of how you feel.

And I have found that motivation often comes AFTER putting in the work that you weren''t motivated to do at first

Like hill sprints

I always walk towards my local hill grumpy and complaining

And the first sprint ALWAYS sucks

Second one also sucks, but a little less

Third one I''m ok

Then during the 4th, 5th, and 6th I''m flying up, feeling invincible

Then I walk back home, motivated to conquer the world

"Pain, uncertainty, and constant work. No matter how successful you become, no one is ever free from them." - Phil Stutz

Click here to check in for Week 38: {Training Magic Link training_slug=week-39-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('e4aa9781-2d7c-5f55-965b-0e558381a99b', 'smf', 39, 'Visualization is a must', null, 'There was a study done once on basketball free throws

They had 3 groups

First group actually practiced free throws for 20 days

Second group did no practice

Third group spent 20 minutes a day visualizing themselves shooting free throws (but didn''t actually do them)

The results were that group 2 saw no improvement

Group 1 saw a 24% improvement

And group 3 saw a 23% improvement

In other words - mental practice yielded results almost identical to physical practice.

This has everything to do with you getting lean and staying there

Your subconscious sets you up with ''automatic behavior'' (your habits)

If you visualize yourself as already being lean, your subconscious will make you act in a way consistent with your mental image.

See yourself already ''there'', put emotion behind it - and you''ll go into automatic pilot mode.

This means, less struggle, less willpower needed

And literally everything you do in regards to you getting lean will become so much easier

Do this today, and then for the rest of your life

The more you do it, the more inevitable your success will become.

Click here to check in for week 39:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-40-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('424cad20-7235-53a3-91e1-f9fdbcc3bf0a', 'smf', 40, 'It''s not all roses when you get lean', null, 'The leaner you get, the more ''visible'' your changes will be

And that''s good

But it can also mess with your mood if you let it

See when you started this journey, you set high standards for yourself

And instead of ''wishing'' for change, you expected greatness

This is how you become a high performer

But your body is unpredictable

Overall, if your calories and protein is right, you will lose weight and improve your health

But SOMETIMES, for inexplicable reasons, you can eat perfect, workout hard, drink water, get sleep

... and still wake up looking ''soft''

Don''t let this get to you

For one, you will always be your own worst critic. And no one else will notice.

Second - if you let minor changes in your appearance get to you, your body owns you and not the other way around

As you get leaner, you must also become mentally resilient enough to not spend emotional energy on your physique having ''mood swings''

It comes with the territory

If you know you''re doing all you can,

You''re sticking to your calories, getting your sleep, lifting heavy, thinking long-term

Those are the biggest levers you can pull

Everything else is minor

And you never want to major in the minors =)

Click here to check in for week 40:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-41-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('e17f1d2a-383e-5498-ae41-ba92aed72d0a', 'smf', 41, 'Time is an illusion', null, 'Aside from having a positive mental attitude, the current moment - the ''now'' - is the most precious thing you will ever have

The ''now'' is the only thing there truly is. The past and present don''t exist.

Your whole entire life is now. There was never a time where your life was not now (nor will there ever be)

The ''now'' is also the only access you have to actually ''live'' and ''be''.

Have you ever had it where you felt totally present? Perhaps you were in a flow state? Or maybe when you went skydiving or did something where you left your comfort zone?

You were living 100% in the ''now'' during that moment

And the good news is that you don''t have to go skydiving to experience this

Focus on the present moment more often. And recognize that your past and present are created by your ego.

Your past and future are not you.

Your past does not define you.

And if you want a better future, you must start incorporating better habits now.

Everything starts now. Because your ''now'' is all you are.

Most people don''t realize this until they are on their death bed.

But like Eckhart Tolle says: "Death is a stripping away of all that is not you. The secret of life is to die before you die."

Click here to check in for week 41:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-42-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('4381bcaf-a09c-5b04-aacc-d0514b42680b', 'smf', 42, 'Do you respect yourself?', null, 'I have a client who''s been going to SUF for over a decade now

He initially lost weight, he''s doing ok, but he''s been completely stuck for years.

The issue is that he drinks every weekend

In case you don''t know, a gram of alcohol = 7 calories

And those are ''empty'' calories

Remember how if you want to be lean, it''s Calories In versus Calories Out?

It''s hard sticking to your ''Calories In'' if you''re drinking 200-300 of them

Where those drinks also increase your appetite and dehydrate you

Alas, alcohol can keep you stuck if you let it

The real issue however is not the alcohol

It''s that he doesn''t respect himself

The hardest respect you''ll ever earn is the respect from yourself

If you want to be awesome

But you keep eating lard and your actions don''t match your words

You might think there''s some secret to turn it all around fast

But, as with everything in life, it''s slow and thorough

Start being that person whom YOU would respect

When you start doing this, nothing will change externally. And it''ll take time.

But your behavior change will slowly change the way you ''see'' yourself

And your destructive habits will die

Only to be replaced with more beneficial ''non-negotiables''

This is how you earn your own respect, and life will be limitless for you.

Click here to check in for week 42:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-43-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('b8d58c7f-5f1f-51f5-bedd-30608cf84ae3', 'smf', 43, 'Raise your Standards', null, '"This is your life, and it''s ending one minute at a time" - so you might as well make the most of it

But instead of just ''going out'' and making things happen, it''s important you set standards for yourself first

Establish for yourself what you find acceptable and unacceptable

You probably don''t realize this, but your biggest internal driver will always be homeostasis

Deep down, your body and mind hate change. It''s human nature to want to remain exactly how you see yourself (ie your standards)

Once you try to elevate from your standards, you will encounter the greatest resistance ever,

This is why many people would rather stay miserable and out of shape, than go through the pain of drinking water and eating healthy.

"This resistance will bury you" - Steven Pressfield

So how do you ''fight'' this resistance?

Answer - you don''t

You flip the script.

You simply raise your standards.

Set your goals so high, so that anything that does NOT meet your high standards aggravates you and is completely unacceptable.

This will form your new reality

And needless to say, this will be tough.

You might lose relationships, but if you do - they were likely holding you down anyway.

And if you''re wondering in what area to raise your standards first...

Obviously, it''s your fitness

Your body and health are what matter most. Without it you have nothing.

Click here to check in for week 43:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-44-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('4e33189c-e372-5b39-9ee3-05ad85995218', 'smf', 44, 'How to raise your testosterone naturally', null, 'Mindset is step #1 in getting lean and living healthy

But instead of talking mindset, let''s talk about your testosterone real quick

Because if your hormones are messed up, no amount of words will make an impact

Testosterone is the #1 hormone that builds muscle and burns fat.

And, it gives you confidence

Here are some tips to raise yours:

-- 1 -- Get enough sleep

7.5 hours ideally (or more)

This will not only kill hunger, it will rebuild your muscle and enhance your recovery

Make sure your room is dark as possible, cold, and minimize sound. Also wind down an hour or two before trying to go to sleep.

-- 2 -- Vitamin D

The only way to get enough is to walk around naked in sunlight all day

So take a 5,000 IU supplement instead

-- 3 -- Get lean and stay lean

Every time you get fat again - you bring your estrogen back up

Estrogen is the opposite of testosterone

-- 4 -- Less workouts (but more intense)

''Burning off'' the fat through exercise will only keep you soft and flabby

It''s counter-intuitive, but the only way to get lean is by cutting calories through food

And then training intense 2-3x week

Overtraining kills testosterone levels

Any time you see someone get lean from working out, they got lean from getting their calories right (either by accident or on purpose)

If most people only put half the time in their kitchen as they do in the gym, they would look like they actually workout (think about that)

-- 5 -- Carbs, Proteins, and Fats

Most of the trendy diets that your coworkers and tia''s talk about rely on cutting out carbs or fats

(most of them cut out carbs, because cutting carbs always leads to an initial 7-10 lbs of weight loss... which is all water)

The problem is, dropping carbs or fats will lower testosterone

And those types of diets aren''t sustainable anyway. Nobody that wants to live a happy life will say no to carbs forever.

Use these 5 tips to keep your testosterone levels high naturally

Not only will this make it easier for you to stay lean, it''ll raise your assertiveness, confidence, and overall sense of well-being

Click here to check in for week 44:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-45-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('d7840cb7-0340-59d7-8395-64dd6c0b555b', 'smf', 45, 'When you go over on your calories', null, 'If you mess up a little and go over on your calories, then do not...

Do NOT...

... beat yourself up and overreact

Accept it, move on, be better the next day

The worst thing you can do is turn it into a binge

Because first off, nobody is perfect all year long

If you''re a human (and not a robot), the goal is to get in shape and stay that way

Make it automatic, turn it into a lifestyle

Being absolutely rigid won''t help with this

Even my best clients mess up and go over their calories once in a while

It never hurts them though

Because they''re on point 90% of the time

If you however let those 500 surplus calories turn into a 4,000 calorie day, you''ll ruin your entire weeks'' progress

And this is where you slow yourself down

Because here you were, sticking to your calories 6 days in a row

Then you messed up on a Saturday, eliminated your entire deficit from the previous week

And now you''re showing up to your gym on Monday, thinking you can somehow ''undo'' your binge in the gym

Sticking to your calories determines whether you have forward momentum in your weight loss

Lose this momentum, and you will quit

You can only go so long without seeing tangible results

Therefore, forget the fitness model rigidity. Stay on point 90% of the time, and don''t beat yourself up if you go over a couple hundred calories

You''re going to be doing this for the rest of your life, so you''ll make up for it in no time

Click here to check in for week 45:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-46-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('b83ebab7-e028-5580-95b9-294467e6924b', 'smf', 46, 'Progress is always exponential', null, 'Mentally we believe that our results should be linear

Meaning, the amount of time we put in should be EQUAL to what we get back

It''s hardwired in our brains (and it kinda makes sense)

But success isn''t like anything else

True success is exponential

You first start working, and nothing happens

It''s like your pumping away at the water pump, and nothing''s coming out

Little do you know, water is rushing it''s way up from deep underneath you

And once it starts flowing, it flows HARD

This is where your results will be 200% better than you expected in the first place

But..

This ''hard flowing'' always comes later than we expect

And 99% of everyone quits before their water starts flowing

Oftentimes, all they needed was 1 or 2 more pumps

Because it''s always darkest before dawn, and failure is a trickster that will trip you right before the finish line

If you understand that initial progress is slow, but speeds up over time - then you''ll be able to stay more motivated

Also, really...

Imagine you just keep going

Really, imagine you simply just keep going?

How on earth will you be in a worse spot next year?

You are bound to succeed

As long as you keep going

Click here to check in for week 46:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-47-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('baec2c89-578b-5eca-85aa-42057979b842', 'smf', 47, 'Nothing is ever easy', null, 'Most people want things to be easy

Especially in losing weight

Lipo wouldn''t even exist if it wasn''t for our desire for comfort and ease

It''s this same desire keeping you overweight

"Pain and Discomfort are like the bouncers of Club Success" - Brandon Carter

Imagine you stopped running from the pain and hard work, and took pride in your ability to withstand discomfort?

You would make losing weight look easy

Thing is, whichever route you choose - you''re going to have problems either way.

It''s just that, when you''re lean and healthy, your problems are more ''luxurious'' in nature

(ie, jealous coworkers, or a partner who can''t keep his/her hands off you)

Whatever you do, only have 1 main goal at a time

And don''t let fear hold you back - be courageous

Courage = acting in spite of fear

So next time you feel a ''fear'' coming up, do it anyway

The courage you''re displaying will instantly give you momentum and inspire others to do the same

Click here to check in for week 47:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-48-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('ae2285da-523a-5841-8c99-958ae6a7619e', 'smf', 48, 'Emotional Motivators', null, 'When you start your fitness journey, all is great

Your motivation is high, whether it be external or intrinsic

But when the initial excitement wears off, you find yourself in the valley of hard work and grit

And it''s here where you will experience emotional struggles.

Your brain wants to survive, so it will ask you:

"Why must I suffer?"

And this is a huge question...

The average person will lack the mental clarity to answer it.

They might have some ''extrinsic'' motivators, but nothing deep enough. So they quit.

Those who truly want it however have dreams, desires, and passions that are burning inside them.

And they have already carefully constructed their ''vision''

So that when their brains ask the question why it must suffer...

Their emotional drivers will push beyond the threshold of pain, to accomplish things that otherwise would be impossible.

Whenever you want to accomplish something big, emotional motivators are the only motivators.

Click here to check in for week 48:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-49-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('ca3013d3-9497-5813-861d-29ce08f13f45', 'smf', 49, 'Learning from your rivals', null, 'So, my favorite soccer team is Ajax Amsterdam

I mean, it''s the ONLY soccer team imo

Our rivals are from the other big Dutch city, Rotterdam

Their team is called Feyenoord Rotterdam (hurts to even type that name)

We at Ajax are known for our bravoure

We''re confident, we have been Dutch champions the most often, and we''ve won the Champions League 4 times

Especially for Dutch standards, we''re pretty arrogant. And the way we play resembles it.

We like to attack and be frivolous in our approach.

Feyenoord on the other hand...

Their club slogan is: "Geen woorden maar daden!"

Literal translation: "No words, just actions!"

They''re more of the blue collar club, for the workers of the harbor.

They roll up their sleeves every game and fight.

Doesn''t have to be pretty, as long as the players work hard - that''s all that matters.

While I''m 100% an Ajax supporter... if I step away and take a 30,000 ft approach

... you can make yourself unstoppable if you combine both these attitudes into one

Be confident (Ajax)

Believe in yourself (Ajax)

Set huge goals (Ajax)

But always put in the work first (Feyenoord)

And drag the results over the finish line, ugly if you have to (Feyenoord)

And only then, are you allowed to speak about what you''ve done (Feyenoord)

I know this is a combination of 2 styles

And your personality will have you sway one way or another

But the moral here is, you can gain knowledge and strength from anyone

Even your fiercest rivals

(even if you absolutely can''t STAND their guts - like Holland & Argentina =))

Click here to check in for week 49:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-50-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('fdc64429-a7b1-54e0-a20f-5cb27d5cba00', 'smf', 50, 'Great ideas are the solution to success', null, 'Don''t ever be afraid of teaching others something you just recently learned

I believe you''re actually meant to ''pass it along''

Even more, I think you''ll only truly ''learn'' something once you teach it to someone else

Reason I''m saying this is because so many people will successfully lose weight, get in shape

And when they see others struggling in their weight loss, they won''t reach out to them because they have ''imposter syndrome''

They feel like ''who are they to teach someone else''?

Truth is, if you have accomplished something that others also want to accomplish - you are almost ethically obligated to share your knowledge

But I get it...

It can feel weird teaching something, if you literally just learned it last month

But, like Dale Carnegie said:

"The ideas I stand for are not mine. I borrowed them from Socrates. I swiped them from Chesterfield. I stole them from Jesus. And I put them in a book. If you don''t like their rules, whose would you use?"

If you''re walking the talk, you''re never an imposter

Your ideas have the power to alter the world

Click here to check in for week 50:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-51-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('7fc5b3fe-bc89-5718-942b-0350682e3c43', 'smf', 51, 'Never let your feelings get in the way (or wait for them)', null, 'Average people wait, wait, wait

They''re always ''getting ready''

Getting ready

Getting ready

They often talk about ''when'' they are going to do what what they want to do

ALL THE WHILE...

The pro''s have already taken imperfect action, and are already out of sight

The difference is that successful people don''t wait for their emotions to motivate them into action.

They master their emotions, because they know if they wait till they ''feel'' like it, they will lose

Because life is finite. Your days are numbered.

Pro''s have a heightened sense of how much longer they have on this earth

And you can always tell by their actions. They take massive action, fast.

So instead of waiting for your mood to strike - simply start. Regardless of how you feel. This small distinctions will mean the difference between ''living your dreams'' and dying with the music still inside you.

"It''s easier to act your way into good thinking than it is to think your way into good action" - Bill Gove

Click here to check in for week 51:
{Training Magic Link training_slug=week-52-habits-and-homework}', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
insert into public.lessons (id, kind, number, title, video_url, body, retired) values ('6a18517d-4529-5f3a-b986-20a2f6de0bc1', 'smf', 52, 'Seek solitude', null, 'Successful people are allowed to be lazy

What I mean is...

The average American watches 1700 hours of TV every year, and spends 1,200 hours on their phone

Both these activities are more of an ''escape'' than a true form of recovery

And because the average waste so much time on meaningless stuff, they never really push themselves beyond the typical routines of everyday life

Successful people know that in order to be at your best, you need proper recovery strategies

And one of the best strategies, is solitude

The simple act of getting away and being quiet creates more space between your thoughts and helps you escape the ''bondage of excessive cognition''

The average person believes more is better. Work harder, harder, hustle, hustle, hustle.

Successful people cut back on traditional work time to let their creative mind operate at greater capacity.

Once you realize the power of a quiet mind, spend some time in solitude.

Your rested mind, body, and spirit will bring out the best of you when it''s time to perform again =)

Click here (https://www.videoask.com/f5zbro61u) to check in for week 52', false) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, video_url = coalesce(public.lessons.video_url, excluded.video_url), body = coalesce(public.lessons.body, excluded.body), retired = excluded.retired;
